1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,238
You'd never expect a pyramid in Antarctica.

2
00:00:02,258 --> 00:00:05,850
But this 1,265-meter peak has four

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:08,052
steep sides. What is it, actually?

4
00:00:08,072 --> 00:00:09,164
That's the first of six

5
00:00:09,184 --> 00:00:11,285
strange things found in Antarctica.

6
00:00:11,305 --> 00:00:14,780
Antarctica attracts a lot of wild theories online.

7
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:16,163
So for each one, we'll look at what

8
00:00:16,183 --> 00:00:18,440
was actually found, and give an honest verdict:

9
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:21,017
solved, best guess, or still unknown.

10
00:00:21,037 --> 00:00:22,420
Number one: the pyramid.

11
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,890
In 2016, pictures of a mountain in

12
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:27,751
Antarctica went viral. Seen from above, it

13
00:00:27,771 --> 00:00:30,208
looked strikingly like an Egyptian pyramid.

14
00:00:30,228 --> 00:00:32,415
Some people said a lost civilization

15
00:00:32,435 --> 00:00:34,588
built it. Others said aliens.

16
00:00:34,608 --> 00:00:36,233
Here's what we know. The peak is in the

17
00:00:36,253 --> 00:00:39,178
Ellsworth Mountains, a range first spotted from a plane

18
00:00:39,198 --> 00:00:42,798
by the American aviator Lincoln Ellsworth in 1935.

19
00:00:42,818 --> 00:00:47,768
It's about 1,265 meters tall, roughly one-fifth the height

20
00:00:47,788 --> 00:00:50,432
of Denali, the tallest mountain in North America.

21
00:00:50,452 --> 00:00:52,332
The Ellsworth Mountains are also known for

22
00:00:52,352 --> 00:00:55,140
fossils of trilobites, sea creatures that lived

23
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,485
more than 500 million years ago.

24
00:00:57,505 --> 00:00:59,105
It doesn't even have an official name.

25
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:00,950
And it isn't hidden. A research camp

26
00:01:00,970 --> 00:01:02,258
sits just south of it.

27
00:01:02,278 --> 00:01:04,547
So how do you get four steep sides? Erosion.

28
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:07,080
Water gets into cracks in the rock, freezes, and

29
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,253
expands. Over and over. The cracks grow, and pieces

30
00:01:10,273 --> 00:01:13,205
break off. Over hundreds of millions of years, that

31
00:01:13,225 --> 00:01:16,013
can carve a mountain into sharp faces.

32
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:18,865
Geologist Eric Rignot put it simply: this is

33
00:01:18,885 --> 00:01:20,960
just a mountain that looks like a pyramid.

34
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,710
He also pointed out that many peaks

35
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:25,186
have one or two faces like that.

36
00:01:25,206 --> 00:01:27,770
Four is rare. But rare isn't impossible.

37
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:29,604
Verdict: solved. It's a mountain.

38
00:01:29,624 --> 00:01:31,819
Number two: the waterfall of blood.

39
00:01:31,839 --> 00:01:34,376
In the McMurdo Dry Valleys, a glacier called

40
00:01:34,396 --> 00:01:37,188
Taylor Glacier has a dark red stain pouring

41
00:01:37,208 --> 00:01:39,541
out of its face. It's called Blood Falls.

42
00:01:39,561 --> 00:01:41,336
It was found in 1911 by the

43
00:01:41,356 --> 00:01:44,251
geologist Thomas Griffith Taylor. Back then, the

44
00:01:44,271 --> 00:01:46,396
red color was blamed on red algae.

45
00:01:46,416 --> 00:01:47,590
But that wasn't it. And there was a

46
00:01:47,610 --> 00:01:50,491
second puzzle. The average temperature there is about

47
00:01:50,511 --> 00:01:53,686
minus seventeen degrees Celsius. So how can anything

48
00:01:53,706 --> 00:01:55,682
flow out of the ice at all?

49
00:01:55,702 --> 00:01:57,465
The answer came from under the glacier.

50
00:01:57,485 --> 00:02:00,284
In 2017, a team used radar to look

51
00:02:00,304 --> 00:02:02,929
through Taylor Glacier. They found a hidden network

52
00:02:02,949 --> 00:02:05,407
of rivers and a lake underneath it, all

53
00:02:05,427 --> 00:02:08,609
filled with very salty, iron-rich water.

54
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:11,553
Salt keeps water from freezing. And when salty water

55
00:02:11,573 --> 00:02:14,805
does start to freeze, it releases heat, which melts

56
00:02:14,825 --> 00:02:17,778
more ice around it. So the water keeps moving.

57
00:02:17,798 --> 00:02:20,432
When this iron-rich water reaches the air, the

58
00:02:20,452 --> 00:02:23,085
iron reacts with oxygen. It turns red, the

59
00:02:23,105 --> 00:02:25,730
same way metal turns red when it rusts.

60
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:28,783
A closer look, reported in 2023, found that

61
00:02:28,803 --> 00:02:31,278
much of the iron sits in tiny round

62
00:02:31,298 --> 00:02:34,647
particles called nanospheres, about a hundredth the size

63
00:02:34,667 --> 00:02:36,130
of a human red blood cell.

64
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:38,974
That makes Taylor Glacier the coldest glacier known

65
00:02:38,994 --> 00:02:41,864
to have water constantly flowing through it.

66
00:02:41,884 --> 00:02:45,120
Verdict: solved. It's salty, iron-rich water,

67
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,691
rusting in the open air.

68
00:02:46,711 --> 00:02:48,631
Number three: the hole in the ice.

69
00:02:48,651 --> 00:02:52,475
Every winter, the sea around Antarctica freezes over.

70
00:02:52,495 --> 00:02:54,925
The sea ice covers an area about twice

71
00:02:54,945 --> 00:02:57,320
the size of the continental United States.

72
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:59,698
Near the coast, openings in the ice are normal.

73
00:02:59,718 --> 00:03:02,505
Strong winds push the ice away from land.

74
00:03:02,525 --> 00:03:06,495
But in 2016 and 2017, a huge hole opened

75
00:03:06,515 --> 00:03:09,039
far out at sea, hundreds of kilometers from the

76
00:03:09,059 --> 00:03:11,880
coast, over water thousands of meters deep.

77
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:13,718
It grew nearly twice the size of

78
00:03:13,738 --> 00:03:16,446
Wales. And it stayed open for weeks.

79
00:03:16,466 --> 00:03:18,553
This kind of opening is called a polynya. This

80
00:03:18,573 --> 00:03:22,048
one formed over an underwater mountain called Maud Rise.

81
00:03:22,068 --> 00:03:24,706
It had been seen before, in the 1970s, when

82
00:03:24,726 --> 00:03:27,244
the first satellites that could see sea ice were

83
00:03:27,264 --> 00:03:30,468
launched. But after that, it showed up only rarely.

84
00:03:30,488 --> 00:03:34,320
Scientists knew that warm, salty water from deep down

85
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:36,998
was rising and melting the ice. But there was

86
00:03:37,018 --> 00:03:40,213
a problem. Melting ice makes the surface water fresher,

87
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:41,696
and that should stop the mixing.

88
00:03:41,716 --> 00:03:42,967
In their words: there must be an

89
00:03:42,987 --> 00:03:45,512
additional input of salt from somewhere.

90
00:03:45,532 --> 00:03:47,808
In 2024, a team from the University

91
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:50,348
of Southampton, the University of Gothenburg, and

92
00:03:50,368 --> 00:03:52,118
UC San Diego found it.

93
00:03:52,138 --> 00:03:53,956
They used satellite maps of the sea

94
00:03:53,976 --> 00:03:56,370
ice, floats drifting in the ocean, and

95
00:03:56,390 --> 00:03:58,315
data from tagged marine mammals.

96
00:03:58,335 --> 00:04:01,336
Swirling currents carried salt onto the top of Maud

97
00:04:01,356 --> 00:04:04,869
Rise. Then a process called Ekman transport, where water

98
00:04:04,889 --> 00:04:07,027
moves at an angle to the wind, pushed that

99
00:04:07,047 --> 00:04:09,597
salt to the spot where the hole first opened.

100
00:04:09,617 --> 00:04:12,105
They called it the essential missing ingredient.

101
00:04:12,125 --> 00:04:15,024
And it matters. Openings like this move huge amounts

102
00:04:15,044 --> 00:04:17,399
of heat and carbon between the ocean and the

103
00:04:17,419 --> 00:04:19,752
air, enough to affect the whole region.

104
00:04:19,772 --> 00:04:23,324
Verdict: solved. Wind, currents, and an underwater mountain.

105
00:04:23,344 --> 00:04:26,714
Number four: the map that shows Antarctica without ice.

106
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:30,309
In 1513, the Ottoman admiral Piri Reis drew a

107
00:04:30,329 --> 00:04:32,999
world map. Only a part of it survives today.

108
00:04:33,019 --> 00:04:36,203
In the 1960s, a writer named Charles Hapgood

109
00:04:36,223 --> 00:04:38,261
claimed the bottom of this map showed the

110
00:04:38,281 --> 00:04:40,737
coast of Antarctica, without its ice. That would

111
00:04:40,757 --> 00:04:43,907
mean someone mapped Antarctica long before anyone was

112
00:04:43,927 --> 00:04:45,014
known to have seen it.

113
00:04:45,034 --> 00:04:47,065
The idea spread through books about

114
00:04:47,085 --> 00:04:50,192
lost civilizations and ancient astronauts.

115
00:04:50,212 --> 00:04:52,969
But historians see it differently. Piri Reis

116
00:04:52,989 --> 00:04:54,651
wrote that he built his map from

117
00:04:54,671 --> 00:04:57,879
older maps, including one by Christopher Columbus.

118
00:04:57,899 --> 00:04:59,292
And the coastline at the bottom can

119
00:04:59,312 --> 00:05:01,649
be explained as South America, bent to

120
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:03,369
fit the edge of the page.

121
00:05:03,389 --> 00:05:06,614
Historian Gregory McIntosh compared it with other maps

122
00:05:06,634 --> 00:05:08,766
from that century. His conclusion: it's not the

123
00:05:08,786 --> 00:05:11,436
most accurate map of the sixteenth century. Many

124
00:05:11,456 --> 00:05:13,782
maps from the same century were far better.

125
00:05:13,802 --> 00:05:17,386
Even Piri Reis's own later map, from 1528,

126
00:05:17,406 --> 00:05:19,682
showed the New World in more detail.

127
00:05:19,702 --> 00:05:21,722
Verdict: solved. It's an early map

128
00:05:21,742 --> 00:05:24,312
of South America, not ice-free Antarctica.

129
00:05:24,332 --> 00:05:26,616
Number five: the lake under the ice.

130
00:05:26,636 --> 00:05:28,549
Deep under the ice sheet, there's a lake about

131
00:05:28,569 --> 00:05:31,713
the size of Lake Ontario. It's called Lake Vostok.

132
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:33,663
It sits under about four kilometers of

133
00:05:33,683 --> 00:05:35,332
ice, and it may have been sealed

134
00:05:35,352 --> 00:05:37,790
off for around fifteen million years.

135
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:39,472
Could anything live down there, in

136
00:05:39,492 --> 00:05:41,337
the dark, under all that pressure?

137
00:05:41,357 --> 00:05:43,199
That's why Lake Vostok is often compared

138
00:05:43,219 --> 00:05:45,894
to Jupiter's moon Europa and Saturn's moon

139
00:05:45,914 --> 00:05:48,909
Enceladus, where oceans may hide under ice.

140
00:05:48,929 --> 00:05:51,675
In February 2012, a Russian team

141
00:05:51,695 --> 00:05:53,933
finally drilled through to the lake.

142
00:05:53,953 --> 00:05:56,608
Scientists had already found microbes in ice

143
00:05:56,628 --> 00:05:58,096
that froze onto the bottom of the

144
00:05:58,116 --> 00:06:00,372
ice sheet, straight from the lake water.

145
00:06:00,392 --> 00:06:03,337
Then in 2013, a study claimed much more.

146
00:06:03,357 --> 00:06:07,674
It reported genetic traces of over 3,500 kinds

147
00:06:07,694 --> 00:06:10,814
of life, including signs of complex animals.

148
00:06:10,834 --> 00:06:13,310
Other experts were skeptical. The old ice cores

149
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:15,950
were drilled to study climate, not life, and

150
00:06:15,970 --> 00:06:19,290
the equipment wasn't sterile. The drilling fluid itself

151
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:21,818
could have carried DNA from the surface.

152
00:06:21,838 --> 00:06:23,551
The study's own leader said: time will

153
00:06:23,571 --> 00:06:25,354
tell if we're right or we're wrong.

154
00:06:25,374 --> 00:06:28,019
Verdict: best guess. Probably some microbes.

155
00:06:28,039 --> 00:06:29,939
The bigger claims are not proven.

156
00:06:29,959 --> 00:06:32,816
Number six: the signals from inside the Earth.

157
00:06:32,836 --> 00:06:36,719
ANITA was a NASA experiment. Radio antennas hung from

158
00:06:36,739 --> 00:06:40,683
a balloon, flying about 37 kilometers above Antarctica.

159
00:06:40,703 --> 00:06:42,683
It listened for radio flashes from

160
00:06:42,703 --> 00:06:44,865
tiny particles hitting the ice.

161
00:06:44,885 --> 00:06:47,735
Most of these are neutrinos. As one physicist put

162
00:06:47,755 --> 00:06:49,893
it, a billion of them are passing through your

163
00:06:49,913 --> 00:06:53,327
thumbnail right now. They almost never interact with anything.

164
00:06:53,347 --> 00:06:56,197
Most signals came down from the sky and bounced

165
00:06:56,217 --> 00:06:58,962
off the ice. But a few did something strange.

166
00:06:58,982 --> 00:07:00,657
They seemed to come up out of the ice,

167
00:07:00,677 --> 00:07:04,176
at steep angles, like 30 degrees below the surface.

168
00:07:04,196 --> 00:07:05,709
To do that, the signal would have had

169
00:07:05,729 --> 00:07:08,599
to travel through thousands of kilometers of rock

170
00:07:08,619 --> 00:07:10,864
first. And the rock should have absorbed it.

171
00:07:10,884 --> 00:07:11,814
Some wondered if this was

172
00:07:11,834 --> 00:07:13,322
a sign of new physics.

173
00:07:13,342 --> 00:07:16,942
So in 2025, scientists checked fifteen years of data

174
00:07:16,962 --> 00:07:19,783
from the Pierre Auger Observatory in Argentina, one of

175
00:07:19,803 --> 00:07:22,378
the biggest particle detectors in the world. If these

176
00:07:22,398 --> 00:07:25,174
events were real particles coming up through the Earth,

177
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:26,894
Auger should have seen many of them.

178
00:07:26,914 --> 00:07:27,476
It didn't.

179
00:07:27,496 --> 00:07:30,208
Physicist Stephanie Wissel said: we still don't actually

180
00:07:30,228 --> 00:07:32,886
have an explanation for what those anomalies are.

181
00:07:32,906 --> 00:07:35,515
A new balloon experiment, called PUEO,

182
00:07:35,535 --> 00:07:37,235
is being built to look again.

183
00:07:37,255 --> 00:07:38,945
Verdict: still unknown.

184
00:07:38,965 --> 00:07:40,523
So, let's check the scoreboard.

185
00:07:40,543 --> 00:07:42,457
The pyramid: solved. A mountain.

186
00:07:42,477 --> 00:07:45,773
Blood Falls: solved. Iron-rich water, rusting.

187
00:07:45,793 --> 00:07:47,412
The hole in the ice: solved.

188
00:07:47,432 --> 00:07:49,895
Wind, currents, and an underwater mountain.

189
00:07:49,915 --> 00:07:52,618
The ice-free map: solved. South America.

190
00:07:52,638 --> 00:07:56,152
Life in Lake Vostok: best guess. Probably microbes.

191
00:07:56,172 --> 00:07:59,098
The signals from inside the Earth: still unknown.

192
00:07:59,118 --> 00:08:01,412
Four solved, one best guess, and one we

193
00:08:01,432 --> 00:08:04,202
still can't explain. Antarctica gets a lot of

194
00:08:04,222 --> 00:08:07,455
wild theories. The real answers are often stranger.

195
00:08:07,475 --> 00:08:10,675
The 3D scenes are illustrations based on the sources.

196
00:08:10,695 --> 00:08:12,258
Which one surprised you the most?

197
00:08:12,278 --> 00:08:13,428
Tell me in the comments.
